# Explicit support floor. Without this file Next uses `defaults`, which resolved to Safari 18.5+
# / Firefox 140+ on the machine that last built — a floor that silently moves every time someone
# runs `npm install` and refreshes caniuse-lite. That is a support policy decided by a lockfile.
#
# Every line below is set by something the app actually ships, not by taste. Raising a floor is
# fine; lowering one means checking the feature that set it.
#
#   Safari / iOS 16.4  — Cesium calls `instanceof OffscreenCanvas` unguarded
#                        (@cesium/engine Scene/Material.js:994 and :1269, no `typeof` check and no
#                        fallback anywhere in the package). Below 16.4 that is a ReferenceError
#                        during widget construction, so the globe does not start at all. This is
#                        the single highest floor in the project and none of it is our code.
#                        `AbortSignal.timeout` (16.0) would otherwise have set it — it ships to
#                        the browser via DestinationSearch.tsx and is a crash, not a degrade.
#
#   Firefox 121        — the `:has()` selector, which hides the map control stack
#                        (globals.css `.app-shell:has(.map-chrome-hidden)`). Firefox shipped
#                        `:has()` late; below 121 the controls stay visible over a globe nobody
#                        is reading. Cosmetic, but it is the highest Firefox floor we have.
#
#   Chrome / Edge 108  — `dvh` units (AppShell's `h-dvh`, the Blue Hour `min-h-dvh` beats).
#                        `:has()` (105) and `AbortSignal.timeout` (103) sit just under it.
#
# Deliberately NOT lowered to chase coverage: every feature above is load-bearing and none has a
# polyfill we would want. Cesium + WebGL2 + a photorealistic tileset is not an experience an old
# browser was going to have.
#
# One feature inside this range is NOT universally available and is used anyway, as a degrade:
#
#   animation-timeline / scroll()  — Chrome 115, Firefox 144, Safari 26.0. So Chrome 108-114,
#                        Firefox 121-143 and Safari/iOS 16.4-18.7 are all in support and all get
#                        no scroll-driven animation. Two things ride it: `.hero-cue`'s fade (which
#                        simply never fades) and `.hero-dusk`'s wash (which never appears, so the
#                        hero-to-ImageRow seam is a hard cut). Both are behind `@supports`, so the
#                        resting state is the unobscured composition rather than a broken one.
#                        This was previously masked above `lg` by a GSAP pin doing the same wash;
#                        that pin is deleted, so the degrade now reaches those browsers' desktop
#                        widths too. Taken knowingly — see DESIGN.md's The Story's One Exit.
#
# Check what this resolves to:  npx browserslist
# Check what it costs:          npx browserslist --coverage

Chrome >= 108
ChromeAndroid >= 108
Edge >= 108
Firefox >= 121
FirefoxAndroid >= 121
Safari >= 16.4
iOS >= 16.4
Samsung >= 21
not dead
